% =======================================================
\section{Initialization and Reference Alignment}
\label{sec:init_alignment}
\label{sec:startup-policy}

Startup keeps attitude initialization outside the MEKF.  The measurement-only
Mahony observer of Sec.~\ref{sec:vertical-proxy} runs from the first sample
while the MEKF remains untouched; wave-period and acceleration-variance
estimators converge in parallel.  The proxy solution initializes the MEKF at a quality or aligned-timeout
handoff. Runtime Live is not by itself a certified P4 entrance.  The accepted handoff is
represented by the compact set $\mathcal H$ and is propagated directly by the
regional theorem of Sec.~\ref{sec:finite-live-capture}.

\subsection{Tilt and operating-point bootstrap}

Before magnetic north is established, yaw is a gauge degree of freedom.  The
proxy initializes roll and pitch from accelerometer direction and evolves by
Eq.~\eqref{eq:proxy-mahony}.  Its low correction bandwidth avoids interpreting
ordinary orbital acceleration as rapid tilt, while the integral term estimates
standing gyro-bias tilt.  Section~\ref{sec:semiglobal-proxy} gives the explicit
practical-ISS estimate used to bound the proxy contribution at handoff.

In parallel, the adaptation front end stages the estimated
$(\tau,\sigma_{aw},r_S)$ operating point.  These are model parameters rather
than state corrections, and the source-reachable schedule family is defined in
Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-source-family}.

\subsection{Quality-gated handoff}
\label{sec:proxy-handoff}

Normal handoff requires: (i) \SI{2}{s} of proxy gravity agreement with
sine-angle error below \num{0.075} on the aligned branch and gyro magnitude
below \SI{30}{\degree\per\second}; (ii) a magnetic gauge when a magnetometer is
present and full-heading operation is requested; and (iii) tuner readiness.  A
minimum \SI{30}{s} startup prevents a quiet initial interval from passing
prematurely.  The Mahony contribution to gate entry is bounded by $T_Q$ in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-quality-entry-time}.

The gravity-agreement test is itself evaluated on an explicitly smoothed
measurement, not on instantaneous specific force.  Each valid accelerometer
sample is first rotated into the current proxy/world frame and then passed
through the source first-order vector EMA
\begin{equation}
 \bar{\vct f}_{k}
 =\bar{\vct f}_{k-1}
 +\alpha_{g,k}\bigl(\vct f^N_k-\bar{\vct f}_{k-1}\bigr),
 \qquad
 \alpha_{g,k}=1-e^{-\Delta t_k/\SI{12}{s}}.
 \label{eq:init-gravity-gate-ema}
\end{equation}
The first valid sample initializes the EMA state directly rather than blending
from zero.  A gate verdict is ignored until this world-frame average has run for
\SI{5}{s}; only then can the separate \SI{2}{s} consecutive-good counter
contribute to handoff.  Both the vector EMA and its elapsed/good-time counters
are reset at outer initialization and when the inner filter re-enters the Cold
startup stage.  These details matter because the \SI{12}{s} EMA removes
zero-mean orbital acceleration before the gravity-direction residual is judged.

The configured proxy timeout is \SI{150}{s}; with the deployed magnetic
acquisition deadline the effective source timeout remains
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-timeout-150}.

At handoff, the proxy attitude seeds the MEKF once.  With magnetic north
established, attitude covariance uses
\[
\sigma_{\mathrm{tilt}}=\SI{0.035}{rad},\qquad
\sigma_{\mathrm{yaw}}=\SI{0.087}{rad};
\]
without a heading gauge, $\sigma_{\mathrm{yaw}}=\pi/2$.  The linear
$[v,p,S,a_w]$ block, current OU parameters, and integral regularizer are enabled
together.  Accelerometer-bias learning remains held until the magnetic
refinement logic releases it.  The initial normal-Live interval therefore uses
the held-bias branch of Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-21-ues}, with the frozen residual
bias entering the active dynamics as a bounded input.

The gravity residual is a sine of alignment angle, so the gate also requires
the positive-inner-product branch of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-aligned-branch}.  The timeout path uses the same branch
test.

For a full-heading handoff, the accepted magnetic or equivalent reference
defines the adopted horizontal gauge.  The gravity gate gives the transverse
attitude bound of Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-tilt}, and the internal
heading error relative to the adopted gauge is bounded by
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-yaw-internal}.  A residual magnetic reference
offset $b_{\psi,\mathrm{ref}}$ contributes to true-north error through
Eq.~\eqref{eq:iss-true-yaw-error} but does not enter the gauge-referenced
stability radius.

The true handoff set must retain one common startup/source history rather
than independent coordinate uncertainties. In particular the fresh nominal
linear states are zero but the session-origin physical primitives generally
are not; see the exact entry graph below. The conditional compact set
$\mathcal H$ of Sec.~\ref{sec:semiglobal-handoff} needs a verified inclusion
of this reachable graph, not a covariance-confidence assumption.  The Live interface condition is
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal H_{k_H}\subset\mathcal D_{k_H},
 \label{eq:init-handoff-safe}
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal D$ is the chart-, covariance-, measurement-, and mode-safe
domain.  Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture} propagates $\mathcal H$ itself
through the exact source-realizable nonlinear map.  Individual early lifts may
expand as long as every prefix remains in $\mathcal D$, and capture occurs when
the propagated set reaches $\mathcal B$ after the finite count $N_H$ of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-count}.

The timeout branch uses the analytical attitude envelope
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-timeout-tilt}.  With a finite heading gauge it defines
$\mathcal H_T\subset\mathcal D$ and uses the same direct capture theorem.  The
source does not require \texttt{north\_ready} at timeout, so an ungauged timeout
instead begins in the quotient safe domain
$\mathcal H_T^\perp\subset\mathcal D^\perp$ and is covered by
Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid-quotient-capture}.  A later magnetic acquisition moves
to the full-heading chain only when the exact jump image and its recovery flow
satisfy Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-entry}.

\subsection{Two-stage magnetic reference}
\label{sec:mag-two-stage}

Magnetic acquisition uses proxy tilt rather than the MEKF.  Raw magnetometer
samples are averaged in a yaw-stripped level frame, preventing arbitrary proxy
yaw from contaminating field direction; the time-based window spans multiple
wave periods to reduce wave-correlated levelling error.

A provisional reference can establish the first horizontal gauge.  A later
independent acquisition refines that reference after the low-bandwidth proxy has
settled.  Acceptance of the refined reference replaces the world magnetic
reference, re-gauges yaw once when a finite acquired yaw is available, and
releases the accelerometer-bias hold.  Tilt and additive navigation variables
remain numerically unchanged; the bias-enable path may apply its bounded
covariance floor.

Section~\ref{sec:hybrid-mag-jump} models either Live magnetic source event by
$\mathcal R_M$.  Because the adopted gauge rotates the complete horizontal
frame, changing it changes the gauge-referenced values of $(v,p,S,a_w)$ even
though their stored source values are unchanged.  The coordinate-reference
jump is given by Eqs.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-world-error-jump}--
\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-world-error-bound}.  The jump must remain inside
$\mathcal D$ and admit the verified recovery sequence of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-entry}.  Continuous hard-iron correction after
refinement writes neither attitude nor covariance and remains in the bounded
normal-Live source family.

\subsection{Live re-lock boundary}

A sustained estimated tilt above $70^\circ$ for \SI{0.35}{s} can trigger a
Live tilt re-lock; a \SI{3}{s} cooldown prevents another reset while its timer
is positive.  The exact source map preserves pre-reset Euler yaw, rebuilds
roll/pitch from the instantaneous accelerometer, reseeds the attitude covariance,
and clears attitude cross-covariances while retaining additive navigation and
bias states.

A deterministic reset guarantee requires the specific-force condition
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-force-bound}; the tilt trigger alone does not impose
that measurement-quality condition.  The source Euler-yaw preservation is
covered on the nonsingular chart of Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-yaw-chart}.  The
reset may enlarge the error, but its image must remain in $\mathcal D$ and its
post-jump flow must satisfy Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-entry}.

For repeated re-locks, the cooldown supplies the dwell time in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-dwell}.  When the jump amplification and shortest
admissible Live flow satisfy
Eq.~\eqref{eq:widen-hybrid-mu},
Theorem~\ref{thm:widen-hybrid-small-gain} gives a practical exponential bound on
the reset-to-reset sequence rather than requiring complete recovery before each
possible reset.

\subsection{Executable fresh-entry graph and reference origin}
\label{sec:exact-live-entry-graph}
For a fresh outer \texttt{begin}, the inner MEKF is constructed with
$\hat v=\hat p=\hat S=\hat a_w=\hat b_g=\hat b_a=0$.
Pre-Live updates advance the private Mahony observer and adaptation states,
not the MEKF motion prediction or its measurement corrections. These zeros
are therefore carried into the first \texttt{goLive}; that method does not
reset previously propagated linear states. A caller which directly drives
the inner MEKF before \texttt{goLive} requires a different reachable graph.
At the fresh boundary $t_L$, before the next completed Live event,
\[
(e_v,e_p,e_S,e_a,e_g,e_b)
=(v_m(t_L),p_m(t_L),S_m(t_L),a_m(t_L),b_g(t_L),\beta(t_L)).
\]
There is no executable truth-position or truth-integral rebase at this boundary.
With the session convention $S_m(t)=\int_0^t p_m(s)\,ds$, neither $e_p=0$
nor $e_S=0$ follows from a zero estimate. Velocity is the instantaneous physical
wave velocity, not a vessel-at-rest hypothesis. Proxy integral feedback is
not copied into the MEKF gyro-bias estimate.

The fresh $v,p,S$ covariance blocks are respectively $I,400I,2500I$, with
zero mutual cross-blocks. These are filter initialization choices, not hard
bounds on physical error. Attitude initialization clears attitude/bias
cross-blocks and the entire base/linear cross-block. Live entry replaces
$P_{aa}$ by the same active stationary OU covariance and clears all its
cross-blocks. Candidate/active tuner history is retained and committed.
The pseudo scheduler has zero accumulated progress while the MEKF is held;
parameter retargeting does not create a prediction tick. Later H18-to-A21
release preserves nominal states and motion covariance, while flooring the
accelerometer-bias covariance diagonal. Subsequent covariance and all
cross-covariances are part of the same-history proof state.

The aligned timeout does not require tuner readiness or a usable measured
period. It can enter Live with the fixed $0.2\,\mathrm{Hz}$ tuner prior.
A finite log-period before the usable latch also leaves this selector on its
prior. Only a valid moment-ratio update can set the one-way latch; that
sample's tuner still uses the predecessor selector. Therefore a theorem that
requires a measured period at every runtime-Live entrance omits a shipping
branch. Quiet BRMM is admitted and must not be excluded solely to force a
period estimate. An end-to-end P5 claim requires finite capture into a P4
basin covering the actual branch, not merely expiration of the timeout.
